  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good as a clear, evolvable envelope substrate, but weak on this scenario because the actual per-hop authorization failure model is delegated to wrapper or payload protocols instead of being specified here.",
    "strengths": [
      "Very clear envelope-vs-payload boundary, including explicit handling of unknown pCIDs.",
      "Deterministic CBOR bytes and mandatory signature slots support later byte-level audit and authenticated hop-local evidence.",
      "No central registry is assumed; opaque store-and-forward behavior helps long-term evolution."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not define ring semantics or what Bob must do when forwarding to Carol is unauthorized.",
      "It does not choose among break-the-ring, skip-the-hop, signed refusal, or membership reconfiguration behavior.",
      "Opaque payload and signature payload formats reduce direct human/LLM comparability across transports."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Different payload protocols could encode authorization failure differently, making bakeoff results non-comparable.",
      "Later auditors may preserve the bytes but still lack understandable refusal semantics if they do not have the payload or signature handlers.",
      "Draft status leaves room for variant drift before any refusal pattern is frozen."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload or wrapper pCID is supposed to carry Bob's signed refusal or non-forward record?",
      "What peer-local accounting record distinguishes an explicit authorization refusal from a silent drop?",
      "Should unauthorized forwarding trigger ring breakage, skipped delivery, or a signed membership-change message in this family?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
